    "title": "LESSON 30: THE VANISHING LETTER",
    "header_color": "yellow",
    "pages": [
      {
        "page": 1,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "   Setelah kesalahan besar minggu lalu,",
          "   perusahaan memecat dua pekerja.",
          "   = After a serious mistake last week, the",
          "     company dismissed two workers.",
          "",
          "The card says pecat. The sentence says",
          "memecat. The p has gone for good.",
          "",
          "This is the rule that makes a dictionary",
          "hard to use. Four letters are swallowed by",
          "meN-, and the rest are not:",
          "",
          "   p  ->  mem-   pecat    memecat     Press 1",
          "   t  ->  men-   tatap    menatap     Press 2",
          "   k  ->  meng-  kirim    mengirim    Press 3",
          "   s  ->  meny-  susun    menyusun    Press 4",
          "",
          "Those four are gone. Everything else stays."
        ],
        "pronunciation_mappings": {
          "1": "Pecat. Memecat.",
          "2": "Tatap. Menatap.",
          "3": "Kirim. Mengirim.",
          "4": "Susun. Menyusun."
        }
      },
      {
        "page": 2,
        "total_pages": 2,
        "content": [
          "   me-    l m n r w y   lacak  melacak",
          "   mem-   b f (p)       balas  membalas",
          "   men-   c d j (t)     cari   mencari",
          "   meng-  g h (k) vowel urus   mengurus",
          "   meny-  (s)           susun  menyusun",
          "   menge- one syllable  cat    mengecat",
          "",
          "The letters in brackets are the four that",
          "vanish. To read backwards, take the prefix",
          "off and try the bracket letter first:",
          "",
          "   memecat   ->  pecat, not ecat",
          "   menatap   ->  tatap, not atap",
          "   mengurus  ->  urus, and there is no k",
          "",
          "Where both readings are real words the",
          "sentence decides, and that is rare. Nothing",
          "in this pack stays ambiguous twice."
        ]
      }
